WEBVTT

00:00:00.673 --> 00:00:02.928
Command of evidence, with data.

00:00:02.928 --> 00:00:05.628
The answer must be accurate and relevant.

00:00:05.628 --> 00:00:08.266
Most wrong answers are only one of those.

00:00:09.811 --> 00:00:11.436
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:11.436 --> 00:00:13.611
You'll see a table or a graph.

00:00:13.611 --> 00:00:17.517
And a question asking which choice uses the data to support a claim.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.445
It's testing whether you can connect data to one specific claim.

00:00:23.502 --> 00:00:24.722
Here's the method.

00:00:24.722 --> 00:00:26.272
Read the claim first.

00:00:26.272 --> 00:00:28.347
What comparison does it need?

00:00:28.347 --> 00:00:32.372
Then read the graphic: the title, the labels, the units.

00:00:32.372 --> 00:00:34.812
For each choice, check it's accurate.

00:00:34.812 --> 00:00:37.262
And check it's relevant to this exact claim.

00:00:38.382 --> 00:00:39.757
Four data rules.

00:00:39.757 --> 00:00:42.507
The answer must be accurate and relevant.

00:00:42.507 --> 00:00:46.317
Match the comparison: the right groups, and the right years.

00:00:46.317 --> 00:00:49.082
Check the units, like percent versus number.

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:51.282
And a change needs two points.

00:00:51.282 --> 00:00:53.066
One year can't show a trend.

00:00:54.093 --> 00:00:55.258
Let's try one.

00:00:55.258 --> 00:00:56.733
Read the claim first.

00:00:56.733 --> 00:01:01.593
It has two parts: biking more than doubled, and walking stayed nearly unchanged.

00:01:01.593 --> 00:01:02.718
Now the table.

00:01:02.718 --> 00:01:05.293
Biking went from one point eight to seven.

00:01:05.293 --> 00:01:08.743
Walking went from five point two to five point six.

00:01:08.743 --> 00:01:11.793
A gives only one year, so it can't show a change.

00:01:11.793 --> 00:01:13.643
C doesn't show either change.

00:01:13.643 --> 00:01:15.568
D covers walking only.

00:01:15.568 --> 00:01:17.622
B shows both parts of the claim.

00:01:18.727 --> 00:01:23.102
Watch out for the choice that's true about the graph, but doesn't prove the claim.

00:01:23.102 --> 00:01:24.652
Accurate isn't enough.

00:01:24.652 --> 00:01:26.087
It has to be relevant.

00:01:27.144 --> 00:01:29.034
Here's the lesson on one page.

00:01:29.034 --> 00:01:30.094
How to spot it.

00:01:30.094 --> 00:01:31.469
The four steps.

00:01:31.469 --> 00:01:32.874
The data rules.

00:01:32.874 --> 00:01:33.924
And the trap.

00:01:33.924 --> 00:01:36.712
Pause the video now, and copy it into your notes.
